.TH PUTC 3S
.SH NAME
putc, putchar, fputc, putw \- put character or word on a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B "#include <stdio.h>"
.sp
.B "int putc(c, stream)"
.B "char c;"
.B "FILE *stream;"
.sp
.B "putchar(c)"
.sp
.B "fputc(c, stream)"
.B "FILE *stream;"
.sp
.B "putw(w, stream)"
.B "FILE *stream;"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B putc
appends the character
.I c
to the named output
.IR stream .
It returns the
character written.
.PP
\fBputchar(\fIc\fB)\fR
is defined as
\fBputc(\fI c,\fB stdout)\fR.
.PP
.B fputc
behaves like
.BR putc ,
but is a genuine function rather than a macro.
It may be used to save on object text.
.PP
.B putw
appends word (i.e.
.BR int )
.I w
to the output
.IR stream .
It returns the
word written.
.B putw
neither assumes nor causes special alignment in the
file.
.PP
The standard stream
.B stdout
is normally buffered if and only if the output
does not refer to a terminal; this default may be changed by
.BR setbuf (3).
The standard stream
.B stderr
is by default unbuffered unconditionally, but
use of
.B freopen
(see
.BR fopen (3))
will cause it to become buffered;
.BR setbuf ,
again, will set the state to whatever is desired.
When an output stream is
unbuffered information appears on the destination file or terminal as
soon as written; when it is buffered many characters are saved up and
written as a block.
.B fflush
(see
.BR fclose (3))
may be used to force the block
out early.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
fopen(3), fclose(3), getc(3), puts(3), printf(3), fread(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
These functions return the constant
.B EOF
upon error.
Since this is a good
integer,
.BR ferror (3)
should be used to detect
.B putw
errors.
.SH BUGS
Because it is implemented as a macro,
.B putc
treats a
.I stream
argument with
side effects improperly.
In particular `putc(c, *f++);' doesn't work sensibly.
.sp
